      "math_academy_id": "q-111301",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "The random variable $X$ has the following probability distribution.\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.5$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet $Y=1-{X}^{3}.$ The distribution of $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $y$ | $b$ | $1$ | $2$ | $a$ | $28$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $c$ | $d$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $a+c?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's add a $Y=1-{X}^{3}$ row to our table:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $28$ | $9$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| ${f}_{X}(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.05$ | $0.5$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distribution of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $9$ | $28$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ | $0.5$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, $a=9,c=0.5,$ and $a+c=9.5.$",
